              "text": "Species",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Reasonably common in dogs.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Species"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Uncommonly reported in cats but",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Species"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "suspected with increasing frequency.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Species"
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Breed Predilections",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Congenital—small to medium dogs; border",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Breed Predilections"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "collies.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Breed Predilections"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Acquired—any medium–large dog; often",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Breed Predilections"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "suspected in working dogs, so frequently",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Breed Predilections"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "German shepherd dogs and Malinois but also",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Breed Predilections"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "common in boxers, Rottweilers.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Breed Predilections"
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Mean Age and Range",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Congenital—3–8 years.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Mean Age and Range"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Acquired—mean age at onset 6–7 years.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Mean Age and Range"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Sacral osteochondrosis signs often appear",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Mean Age and Range"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "~1 year old.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Mean Age and Range"
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Predominant Sex",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Congenital—none.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Predominant Sex"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Acquired—male.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1,
              "subheading": "Predominant Sex"
            }
          ]
        },
        "SIGNS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNS",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Lumbosacral pain—salient feature; may be the only clinical sign; may be evident in reluctance to jump or climb stairs. Pain on pressure on, or dorsiflexion of, the lumbo sacral vertebral column (often also induced during extension of hip joints).",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Pelvic limb lameness—caused by lumbar 7th and/or sacral nerve dysfunction; may progress to pelvic limb weakness, muscle wasting, and postural reaction deficits.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Urinary and/or fecal incontinence—caused by S1–3 nerve dysfunction.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Abnormal tail carriage, tail weakness or paralysis—results from dysfunction of caudal nerves.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Self-inflicted lesions—most often associated with congenital lesions or cauda equina inflammation.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "CAUSES": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CAUSES",
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Congenital vertebral malformation, including transitional vertebrae, or osteochondrosis of the cranial sacrum.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Intervertebral disc herniation (types I and II).",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hypertrophy or hyperplasia of the interarcuate ligament.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Proliferation of the articular facets and/or periarticular soft tissues.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Subluxation at the lumbosacral junction.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Inflammatory or neoplastic disease of the vertebral canal can produce identical clinical signs (hence “cauda equina syndrome”).",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "RISK FACTORS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "RISK FACTORS",
          "canonical_heading": "RISK FACTORS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Dogs with lumbosacral transitional vertebrae, especially German shepherd dogs, have increased risk to develop the syndrome.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
