        "APPROPRIATE HEALTH CARE": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "APPROPRIATE HEALTH CARE",
          "canonical_heading": "APPROPRIATE HEALTH CARE",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Urinary incontinence—inpatient for initial management.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "NURSING CARE": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "NURSING CARE",
          "canonical_heading": "NURSING CARE",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Urinary incontinence—manual expression or catheterize the bladder until adequate voluntary control returns; monitor for urinary tract infection and administer appropriate antibiotics following culture and sensitivity.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "ACTIVITY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "ACTIVITY",
          "canonical_heading": "ACTIVITY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Nonsurgical treatment—confinement and restricted leash walks, alone or combined with systemic anti-inflammatory drugs or analgesics, or epidural injection of corticosteroids, frequently alleviate pain; clinical signs may return with increasing levels of exercise.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "If treated surgically, restrict for 6–12 weeks; then gradual return to athletic function.",
              "pdf_page": 923,
              "printed_page": 841,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "DIET": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIET",
          "canonical_heading": "DIET",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Avoid obesity; excess weight increases biomechanical stress on the spine.",
              "pdf_page": 924,
              "printed_page": 842,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "CLIENT EDUCATION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CLIENT EDUCATION",
          "canonical_heading": "CLIENT EDUCATION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Inform client that there may be progressive neurologic impairment of the pelvic limbs, urinary and fecal incontinence, and paralysis of the tail.",
              "pdf_page": 924,
              "printed_page": 842,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Inform client that pelvic limb lameness and self-inflicted lesions result from pain associated with nerve root irritation and/or compression.",
              "pdf_page": 924,
              "printed_page": 842,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Discuss surgical treatment, which is appropriate for cases with severe pain and may be suitable for cases with neurologic deficits (especially incontinence, which may be difficult to reverse).",
              "pdf_page": 924,
              "printed_page": 842,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Medical management is frequently successful for animals with mild pain and/or mild neurologic deficits only.",
              "pdf_page": 924,
              "printed_page": 842,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SURGICAL CONSIDERATIONS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SURGICAL CONSIDERATIONS",
          "canonical_heading": "SURGICAL CONSIDERATIONS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Several options, each focused on alleviating the specific source of the problems defined by imaging; dorsal laminectomy, lateral foraminotomy, or fixation–fusion may all be effective in specifically selected cases.",
              "pdf_page": 924,
              "printed_page": 842,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
